hi there steve kaufman another glorious  day here in vancouver  and uh again i'm going to talk about  language learning of course but i'm  going to talk about language learning  and money  and it's a subject that sometimes people  don't like to associate with learning or  with education the idea of money  money  kind of makes learning dirty education  should be free  language learning should be free  everything on the internet should be  free  so there's this tremendous sort of  anti-commercial bias  in our society and it's not just in our  society it's not just a matter of  teachers or universities today and  culticating the idea that everything  should be free free is morally good  anything that has a dollar sign attached  to it is bad that's been around since  the beginning of time  i think in ancient china and in europe  you know the priests and the soldiers  and the rulers and the aristocracy were  considered much higher on the totem pole  than the money-grubbing merchants  but in reality whenever  culture has flourished the arts have  flourished music  good food  new technology all of the kinds of  things advances in medicine these have  always taken place  during periods when  commerce flourished and it was the  merchants who made things happen the  merchants who were i mean tang china  which was this great flowering of of  culture and printing and the arts  uh was also a period of great commercial  activity the silk road  rice moving from southern china to the  north  uh hangzhou great commercial center  you know active traders from  uh from central asia and um  commercial contacts with korea and  and and japan and so forth and it was  the same in uh  you know florence uh or at least in if  you look in in so far as the uh  you know the late middle ages and the  development of the renaissance it was  commercial centers like flanders  and tuscany and and the other cities in  italy that  that spurred this great you know  cultural and  literary and philosophical and musical  flowering  so uh i always  kind of have difficulty with the fact  that the the soldiers and the priests  you know genghis khan is glorious  because he killed lots of people but the  merchants are just money grubbers so  that's just to get things started and  many of the things that we enjoy in life  again food the farmer likes to get paid  um  you know historically artists have been  supported by people with money  people have no money can't support an  artist  so get us back to education  a good teacher  or a person who writes  a very good textbook  um  people who create  rosetta stone whatever  all those people need to be compensated  for  their efforts  um  you know when i went to study romanian i  found people through elance who would  translate  uh english sentences phrases patterns  that i wanted into romanian and record  them for me i paid them they wouldn't do  it for free  now i'm not against obviously  volunteerism  uh we had big floods in the city of  calgary in canada  and lots of people helped their  neighbors there was a great  sort of attitude of mutual help  but those people who were helping their  neighbors  they all have jobs  because they all nevertheless need to  earn in order to buy the things that  they need so let's bring this back to  language learning every time i learn a  language i spend money  i buy books or i buy the services of a  tutor i buy the services of someone who  can help me by translating or  transcribing  uh  and yet i also take advantage of  whatever is free  i listen to podcasts i download  podcasts and transcripts so  i'm not obliged to spend money  but because i enjoy language learning  i do spend money and money is an  extremely useful invention because  without money  if i didn't have the means of sending  via paypal  money to romania to have  uh this material transcribed for me or  recorded for me  i couldn't have it done i couldn't say  uh  i will take out your garbage in romania  for a week or i will cut your lawn or i  will do some service for you i'll give  you a massage  once a day for seven days there's no  service that i can provide  uh it's far more convenient for me to  simply say how much do you want  so that that person gets some money that  they then can spend in however they want  to spend it so money liberates you  to  earn for your  efforts and skills and labor  a medium exchange that you can take  somewhere else and buy things that  matter to you  now  learning should be free education should  be free language learning should be free  why  it's something that i value  i value  learning languages more than buying a  starbucks coffee  so i'm happy to spend money on a book  in czech so i can read about the history  of czechoslovakia other people don't  want to do that they'll spend their  money on  coffee cigarettes  hiking boots skiing trips whatever it  might be  but since learning is of value and of  course we spend a fortune on education  we spend a fortune  between government spending and private  spending  in  most countries at seven eight nine ten  percent of gdp it's a fortune and the  only decision that has to be made is  whether how much of that spending should  be done by the individual  the  in the individual consumer of those  educational services and how much  should be paid for by the community at  large in the form of taxes or other  means of supporting these institutions  including donations  but all of these are rational decisions  that people have to make and you don't  have to spend money to learn languages  you can go to the library and you can  borrow  whatever you need and you can meet with  people you don't have to meet for a  coffee you can meet in the park if you  can somehow contact  people  who have who speak the language that you  so you have these meetups that are  organized through the internet but even  the internet even google all of those  things there is a mel an element where  those  um people behind google have to make  money to pay their employees so you  can't escape from the commercial  aspects of all of these activities and  there is nothing wrong with people  providing a service  for  a return and it's up to the consumer to  decide  whether link is worth it for example or  not worth it rosetta stone is worth it  or not worth it uh  signing up for a course at a local  community college is worth it or not  worth it those are decisions that each  person makes as a consumer just as they  decide whether they want to buy a big  car or a small car now there is a  problem with money  because money is identified with power  and so the problem in a sense is  inequality so we have inequality of  money we have inequality of possessions  we have inequality of power in a society  and that's true of any society including  communist so-called egalitarian  societies inequality is always there of  course  the less unequal society is the more  comfortable a place usually it is to  live in all other things being equal  however is if  inequality or excuse me if equality is  imposed  by means of drastic restrictions of  freedom  drastic restrictions of the freedom to  produce products to own your own home to  own your own farm for farming purposes  in other words if we look at some of  these idealistic experiments  which led to totalitarian governments  where  individual freedom was restricted  the results were not so happy so there  is no perfect world there is no  perfectly equal society there is no  society where everybody just  gives to everyone else because the  nature of human beings is that we do  have this tendency to look after our own  interests  and  the use of money as a medium of exchange  whereby  someone who's interested in my services  will pay me for my for what i provide  and i will take the money and then use  it to buy things that are of interest to  me that seems to work well so i guess  what i'm saying is i don't understand  this tremendous aversion to quote  commercialism in education  education is extremely important in our  society the greater variety of  educational services that are available  for people to choose from the better  some will be financed by government  some will not be financed by government  not every  educational service offered by the  government is necessarily effective good  value or useful so  by all means government  should  provide educational services as a means  of mitigating against inequality in a  society and i 100 support the idea of  publicly funded schooling right up to  grade 12.  would even support publicly funded  schooling right up through university if  there were  total freedom of choice but the way  things work right now  universities have become sort of  bastions of privilege for the people who  work in the system  and for those who are lucky enough to  get in  so i don't think that's such a great  model i would rather see a model where  the state financed  uh the sort of distribution of  educational services free to everyone  via the internet uh or whatever other  means without having this tremendous  cost of supporting  uh  this this very heavy uh infrastructure  of teachers and administrators and so  forth there are that are associated with  public education today public funding  yes a more decentralized and efficient  way of delivering educational services  would be better so i've kind of rambled  a bit i wanted to get this off my chest  i look forward to your feedback thank  you for listening bye for now
 

